#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171761 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8157" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ ∗ & -\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$, the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that\n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1$.\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-8$. Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D=P^T \\! A P$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.** Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=5\\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\lambda_2=-8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5$, we have\n$A - 5I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - 5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{13}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8$, we have\n$A - (-8)I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} + 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{13}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP$, where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A$.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=1\n$$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-1)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + I &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_2$. Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nDividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d219f0f-39c4-5862-af5d-12791bde950c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171218} {:answer/id #uuid "b45cb8f7-107e-57d7-bcce-5f7edddf8eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171219} {:answer/id #uuid "e4450e4a-a83d-5d96-a4c2-72571169f71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171220} {:answer/id #uuid "31964a19-89f9-5567-b61a-aeb0b9c5d51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171221} {:answer/id #uuid "56a62c21-c273-5af3-b6a7-6447609edb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4450e4a-a83d-5d96-a4c2-72571169f71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171220} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ddc9ab7-6d81-5c34-adce-3d51172ec885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171217}] :question/id #uuid "f738fa7a-a5ce-518d-9ada-841b441fb202" :question/math-academy-id "q-94056" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 4$ and $λ_{2} =-2$. Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{T}AP$."}]}
